{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2469 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "80c60ab168aa228d033e7f2174eda58db077c3bd4357f8dedefeba4101d3d97a" :edb/db-before-t 2468 :edb/db-after-t 2469 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535781 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:07:13.922Z" 13194139535781 true] [17592186124173 1100 "Given the function $f(x,y) = 6x^2+2y^2 - 2xy^2$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (3,3)$." 13194139535781 false] [17592186124173 1100 "Given the function $f(x,y) = 6x^2+2y^2 - 2xy^2,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (3,3).$" 13194139535781 true] [17592186124173 1145 "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = k\n$$\n\nat the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0 \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (3,3)\n$$\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } \\, f ( x , y ) & = {f}_{x} ( x , y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} + {f}_{y} ( x , y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x} ( 6 {x}^{2} + 2 {y}^{2} - 2 x {y}^{2} ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y} ( 6 {x}^{2} + 2 {y}^{2} - 2 x {y}^{2} ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = ( 12 x - 2 {y}^{2} ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} + ( 4 y - 4 x y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{\\nabla } \\, f ( 3 , 3 ) & = ( 12 ( 3 ) - 2 ( 3 {)}^{2} ) \\mathbf{i} + ( 4 ( 3 ) - 4 ( 3 ) ( 3 ) ) \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = 18 \\, \\mathbf{i} - 24 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = \\langle  18 , - 24 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the level curve that passes through the point\n\n$$\n(x_0,y_0)=(3,3)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x_{0}, y_{0}) \\cdot \\langle x - x_{0}, y - y_{0}\\rangle  &= 0 \\\\\n\\nabla f(3, 3) \\cdot \\langle x - 3, y - 3\\rangle  &= 0 \\\\\n\\langle 18,-24\\rangle  \\cdot \\langle x - 3, y - 3\\rangle  &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can leave our answer in this form. Or, if we want to leave our final answer in Cartesian form, we expand the dot product and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18(x - 3) - 24(y - 3) &= 0 \\\\\n18x - 24y + 18 &= 0 \\\\\n3x - 4y + 3 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1940/Source/Images/c5004ab213a6d5c7a2ffee17af5d78e1.png)" 13194139535781 false] [17592186124173 1145 "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (3,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(12x-2{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4y-4xy)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,3) & =(12(3)-2(3)^{2})i+(4(3)-4(3)(3))j \\\\  & =18\\,i-24\\,j \\\\  & =⟨18,-24⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the level curve that passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)=(3,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(3,3)\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨18,-24⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can leave our answer in this form. Or, if we want to leave our final answer in Cartesian form, we expand the dot product and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18(x-3)-24(y-3) & =0 \\\\ 18x-24y+18 & =0 \\\\ 3x-4y+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/assets/102f55fe0add794d3ee0f9f044b5e35c4403d4f160fe36e97d737d33ef3abb68.png)" 13194139535781 true] [17592186124174 1100 "Consider the plane curve $x^2-3xy+4y=2$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1)$." 13194139535781 false] [17592186124174 1100 "Consider the plane curve $x^2-3xy+4y=2.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" 13194139535781 true] [17592186124174 1145 "First, if we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2-3xy+4y\n$$\n\nour curve can be viewed as the level curve\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2\n$$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1) = f_y(1,1) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(1,1) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{t} ( x , y ) & = \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y} ( {x}^{2} - 3 x y + 4 y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x} ( {x}^{2} - 3 x y + 4 y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = ( - 3 x + 4 ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - ( 2 x - 3 y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathbf{t} ( 1 , 1 ) & = ( - 3 ( 1 ) + 4 ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - ( 2 ( 1 ) - 3 ( 1 ) ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = 1 \\, \\mathbf{i} - ( - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf i +\\mathbf j$, as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1940/Source/Images/5aaa7192901cc929e9c7816c7f43aa6f.png)" 13194139535781 false] [17592186124174 1145 "First, if we set $f(x,y) = x^2-3xy+4y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1) = f_y(1,1) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(1,1) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(-3x+4)\\text{ }i-(2x-3y)\\text{ }j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(-3(1)+4)\\text{ }i-(2(1)-3(1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =1\\,i-(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf i +\\mathbf j,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/assets/0114bf524d085402bd27178d687a5faf176b7147c8e8726b84042b17823b6b0b.png)" 13194139535781 true] [17592186124175 1100 "At which points on the curve $6xy+3y^2 =7+ y - 3x^2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}$?" 13194139535781 false] [17592186124175 1100 "At which points on the curve $6xy+3y^2 =7+ y - 3x^2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}?$" 13194139535781 true] [17592186124175 1145 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6xy + 3y^{2} &= 7 + y - 3x^{2} \\\\\n6xy + 3y^{2} - y + 3x^{2} &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 6xy+3y^2 - y + 3x^2\n$$\n\nour curve can be viewed as the level curve\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=7\n$$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y) = f_y(x,y) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x,y) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{t} ( x , y ) & = {f}_{y} ( x , y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - {f}_{x} ( x , y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y} ( 6 x y + 3 {y}^{2} - y + 3 {x}^{2} ) \\, \\mathbf{i} - \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x} ( 6 x y + 3 {y}^{2} - y + 3 {x}^{2} ) \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = ( 6 x + 6 y - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} - ( 6 y + 6 x ) \\, \\mathbf{j}\\end{array}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j}$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t \\cdot \\mathbf j = 0\n$$\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{t} ( x , y ) \\cdot  \\mathbf{j} & = 0 \\\\ ( ( 6 x + 6 y - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} - ( 6 y + 6 x ) \\, \\mathbf{j} ) \\cdot  \\mathbf{j} & = 0 \\\\ - 6 y - 6 x & = 0 \\\\ y & = - x\\end{array}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6xy + 3y^{2} &= 7 + y - 3x^{2} \\\\\n6x(-x) + 3(-x)^{2} &= 7 + (-x) - 3x^{2} \\\\\n- 6x^{2} + 3x^{2} &= 7 - x - 3x^{2} \\\\\nx &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if $x=7$, we get $y=-7$ and the corresponding point is $(7,-7)$." 13194139535781 false] [17592186124175 1145 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = 6xy+3y^2 - y + 3x^2 ,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y) = f_y(x,y) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x,y) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf t \\cdot \\mathbf j = 0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -6y-6x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6x(-x)+3(-x)^{2} & =7+(-x)-3{x}^{2} \\\\ -6{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =7-x-3{x}^{2} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if $x=7,$ we get $y=-7$ and the corresponding point is $(7,-7).$" 13194139535781 true] [17592186124372 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365951 13194139535781 true] [17592186124372 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365958 13194139535781 true] [17592186124372 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365965 13194139535781 true] [17592186124372 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366007 13194139535781 true] [17592186124372 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366014 13194139535781 true] [17592186124373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365972 13194139535781 true] [17592186124373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365979 13194139535781 true] [17592186124373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366000 13194139535781 true] [17592186124373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366021 13194139535781 true] [17592186124373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366049 13194139535781 true] [17592186124374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365986 13194139535781 true] [17592186124374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365993 13194139535781 true] [17592186124374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366028 13194139535781 true] [17592186124374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366035 13194139535781 true] [17592186124374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366042 13194139535781 true] [17592186365951 1017 #edb/ref 17592186365952 13194139535781 true] [17592186365951 1089 "q-130094" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365951 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535781 true] [17592186365951 1100 "Given the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-x+y,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1).$" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365951 1113 #uuid "7c1bed00-c1da-539a-bd7b-b11b7bf684ae" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365951 1145 "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-x+y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }i+(2xy+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =((-1)^{2}-1)\\,i+(2(1)(-1)+1)\\,j \\\\  & =0\\,i-j \\\\  & =⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365953 13194139535781 true] [17592186365952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365954 13194139535781 true] [17592186365952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365955 13194139535781 true] [17592186365952 100