b87a-6693662432ef" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365986 1017 #edb/ref 17592186365987 13194139535781 true] [17592186365986 1089 "q-130217" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365986 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535781 true] [17592186365986 1100 "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2y-2x$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $-i+j?$" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365986 1113 #uuid "6851bbc7-80d6-5dc9-8a00-f559624fa12f" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365986 1145 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $-i+j,$ we must have $t\\cdot (-i+j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ -(2y-2)-(2x+2) & =0 \\\\ -2y-2x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-x)^{2} & =2(-x)-2x \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=2,$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,2).$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365987 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365988 13194139535781 true] [17592186365987 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365989 13194139535781 true] [17592186365987 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365990 13194139535781 true] [17592186365987 1000 #e