 & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+8x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\,j \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy+8)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j,$ we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy+8)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -(2xy+8) & =0 \\\\ xy+4 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{4}{x}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}(-\\frac{4}{x}) & =-8x-4 \\\\ -4x & =-8x-4 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $x=-1,$ we get $y=4,$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,4).$" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365994 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365995 13194139535781 true] [17592186365994 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365996 13194139535781 true] [17592186365994 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365997 13194139535781 true] [17592186365994 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365998 13194139535781 true] [17592186365994 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365999 13194139535781 true] [17592186365994 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535781 true] [17592186365994 1041 "selection" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365994 1104 #edb/ref 17592186365999 13194139535781 true] [17592186365994 1110 #uuid "587ce2f9-f1b8-54d6-a699-faadf1247009" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365995 1034 "(1,-12)" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365995 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535781 true] [17592186365995 1111 #uuid "479f22f8-a0db-57b0-b2d1-91061253cab3" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365996 1034 "(-2,3)" 13194139535781 true] [17592186365996 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535781 true] [17592186365996 1111 #uuid "d41105d4-a62c-5ff7-92a9-7