2 1145 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy & =-5y+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy+5y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)j \\\\  & =(2x+5)\\,i-(2x+2y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((2x+5)\\,i-(2x+2y)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -(2x+2y) & =0 \\\\ x & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-y$ into the equation of our curve gives:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4 \\\\ (-y)^{2}+2(-y)y+5y & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+5y & =4 \\\\ -{y}^{2}+5y-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-4) & =0 \\\\ y & =1\\,\\text{or}\\,y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=1$, we get $x=-1$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,1).$\n\n- If $y=4$, we get $x=-4$ and the corresponding point is $(-4,4).$" 13194139535781 true] [17592186366043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366044 13194139535781 true] [17592186366043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366045 13194139535781 true] [17592186366043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366046 13194139535781 true] [17592186366043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366047 13194139535781 true] [17592186366043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366048 13194139535781 true] [17592186366043 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535781 true] [17592186366043 1041 "selection" 13194139535781 true] [17592186366043 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366044 13194139535781 true] [17592186366043 1110 #uuid "a9ddc5cd-e881-5d2a-95bb-6d203811d244" 13194139535781 true] [17592186366044 1034 "$(-1,1)$ and  $(-4,4)$ only" 13194139535781 true] [17592186366044 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535781 true] 